.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt STAT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm stat
.Nd display file or file system status
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm stat
.Op Fl LfthV
.Op Fl c Ar FORMAT
.Op Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Ar FORMAT
.Op Fl Fl printf Ns = Ns Ar FORMAT
.Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility displays information about files or file systems.
By default it shows file status; with
.Fl f
it shows file system status.
.Pp
This implementation follows the GNU interface, in which
.Fl f
and
.Fl t
mean something different than they do in the BSD
.Nm
found on macOS and
.Ox .
See
.Sx CAVEATS .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl L , Fl Fl dereference
Follow symbolic links.
By default
.Nm
reports on the link itself, not the file it refers to.
.It Fl f , Fl Fl file-system
Display file system status instead of file status.
.It Fl c Ar FORMAT , Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Ar FORMAT
Use the specified
.Ar FORMAT
instead of the default, outputting a newline after each
use of FORMAT.
.It Fl Fl printf Ns = Ns Ar FORMAT
Like
.Fl Fl format
but interpret backslash escapes and do not output a
mandatory trailing newline.
.It Fl t , Fl Fl terse
Print the information in terse form.
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Display a help message and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl Fl version
Output version information and exit.
.El
.Pp
The valid format sequences for files (without
.Fl f ) :
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Li %a
Access rights in octal.
.It Li \&%A
Access rights in human readable form.
.It Li %b
Number of blocks allocated.
.It Li \&%B
The size in bytes of each block reported by %b.
.It Li %d
Device number in decimal.
.It Li \&%D
Device number in hex.
.It Li %f
Raw mode in hex.
.It Li %F
File type.
.It Li %g
Group ID of owner.
.It Li %G
Group name of owner.
.It Li %h
Number of hard links.
.It Li %i
Inode number.
.It Li %m
Mount point.
.It Li %n
File name.
.It Li \&%N
Quoted file name with dereference if symbolic link.
.It Li %o
Optimal I/O transfer size hint.
.It Li %s
Total size, in bytes.
.It Li %t
Major device type in hex, for device special files.
.It Li \&%T
Minor device type in hex, for device special files.
.It Li %u
User ID of owner.
.It Li \&%U
User name of owner.
.It Li %w
Time of file birth, human-readable; - if unknown.
.It Li %W
Time of file birth, seconds since Epoch; 0 if unknown.
.It Li %x
Time of last access, human-readable.
.It Li %X
Time of last access, seconds since Epoch.
.It Li %y
Time of last data modification, human-readable.
.It Li %Y
Time of last data modification, seconds since Epoch.
.It Li %z
Time of last status change, human-readable.
.It Li %Z
Time of last status change, seconds since Epoch.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std stat
.Sh EXAMPLES
Display file information:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ stat /etc/passwd
  File: /etc/passwd
  Size: 6804      Blocks: 16         IO Block: 4096   regular file
Device: 1000004h/16777220d  Inode: 3541246     Links: 1
Access: (0644/-rw-r--r--)  Uid: (    0/    root)   Gid: (    0/   wheel)
Access: 2024-01-15 10:30:45.000000000 -0500
Modify: 2024-01-14 09:20:30.000000000 -0500
Change: 2024-01-14 09:20:30.000000000 -0500
 Birth: 2023-06-01 12:00:00.000000000 -0500
.Ed
.Pp
Show only the file size:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ stat -c '%s' /etc/passwd
6804
.Ed
.Pp
Show file type and permissions:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ stat -c '%F %A' /etc/passwd
regular file -rw-r--r--
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ls 1 ,
.Xr test 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is not specified by POSIX.
The behavior is compatible with GNU coreutils.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
.Sh CAVEATS
BSD and GNU
.Nm
assign different meanings to
.Fl f
and
.Fl t .
This implementation follows GNU, so a script written for the
BSD
.Nm
found on macOS and
.Ox
will behave differently:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f
BSD treats the next argument as a format string.
Here it selects file system status and takes no argument.
Use
.Fl c Ar FORMAT
for the BSD
.Fl f Ar FORMAT
behavior.
.It Fl t
BSD treats the next argument as an
.Xr strftime 3
time format.
Here it selects terse output and takes no argument.
There is no equivalent for the BSD behavior.
.El
.Pp
Because these are ordinary flags rather than errors, a BSD
invocation such as
.Ql stat -f '%m' file
does not do what a BSD user expects: the format string is
treated as a file operand.
It is diagnosed and the exit status is non-zero, so the
mistake is not silent.
.Pp
The BSD flags whose letters GNU does not use
.Pq Fl F , l , n , q , r , s , x
keep their BSD meanings here, so only
.Fl f
and
.Fl t
diverge.
